egb339-weeks-1-6-robotics-foundations.body:
> [!abstract] Learning path
> `Linear algebra → SO(2)/SE(2) → SO(3)/SE(3) → pose graphs → forward kinematics → inverse kinematics → Jacobian → differential motion`

## Week-by-week map

1. [Week 1 - Unit introduction and kinematics context](/egb339/uker/uke-1) establishes the robotics and assessment context.
2. [Week 2 - Linear algebra and 2D pose](/egb339/uker/uke-2) introduces [Linear algebra for robotics](/egb339/temaer/linear-algebra-for-robotics), [SO(2) rotation matrices](/egb339/temaer/so-2-rotation-matrices) and [SE(2) homogeneous transformations](/egb339/temaer/se-2-homogeneous-transformations).
3. [Week 3 - NumPy and 3D pose](/egb339/uker/uke-3) extends the model to [SO(3) rotation matrices](/egb339/temaer/so-3-rotation-matrices), [SE(3) homogeneous transformations](/egb339/temaer/se-3-homogeneous-transformations), [Rotation composition in 3D](/egb339/temaer/rotation-composition-in-3d) and [Pose graphs](/egb339/temaer/pose-graphs).
4. [Week 4 - Forward kinematics](/egb339/uker/uke-4) uses [Kinematic chains and joints](/egb339/temaer/kinematic-chains-and-joints) to calculate the end-effector pose.
5. [Week 5 - Inverse kinematics](/egb339/uker/uke-5) maps desired targets back to joint values and introduces [atan2 and inverse trigonometry](/egb339/temaer/atan2-and-inverse-trigonometry) plus [Robot workspace and IK solution branches](/egb339/temaer/robot-workspace-and-ik-solution-branches).
6. [Week 6 - Robot Jacobian](/egb339/uker/uke-6) differentiates FK to connect joint and end-effector velocity through [Robot Jacobian](/egb339/temaer/robot-jacobian) and [Differential kinematics and local linearisation](/egb339/temaer/differential-kinematics-and-local-linearisation).

## The central dependency

[Reference frames](/egb339/temaer/reference-frames) determines what coordinates mean. Rotation and transformation matrices move information between frames. Pose graphs organise the transformations. FK follows the chain from joints to end effector; IK solves the geometry in the opposite direction. The Jacobian differentiates the same FK model to describe local motion and velocity.

## Applied system

[Dobot Magician](/egb339/temaer/dobot-magician) is the physical robot model, and [CoppeliaSim](/egb339/temaer/coppeliasim) provides simulation-based validation. [Assessment 2.1 - Simulation and oral demonstration](/egb339/vurderinger/assessment-2-1-simulation-and-oral-demonstration) combines frame transformation, IK and motion interpolation in simulation; [Assessment 2.2 - Physical robot submission](/egb339/vurderinger/assessment-2-2-physical-robot-submission) transfers the controller to hardware with different geometry and safety constraints.

See also [Position, orientation and robot kinematics](/egb339/temaer/position-orientation-and-robot-kinematics), [Robot word typing practical](/egb339/ressurser/robot-word-typing-practical) and [Keyboard coordinate mapping and safe key presses](/egb339/temaer/keyboard-coordinate-mapping-and-safe-key-presses).

## Common failure modes

- wrong frame direction or a missing inverse;
- incorrect 3D rotation order;
- `*` used instead of `@` in NumPy;
- hard-coded example data rather than general function logic;
- mixed units or angle conventions;
- only one IK branch or the wrong inverse-trigonometric quadrant;
- a Jacobian evaluated at the wrong configuration or inverted at a singularity;
- a local linear approximation applied across an overly large step;
- simulation and physical keyboard or tool parameters mixed together;
- lateral stylus motion before a safe clearance height.

joint-angle-mapping.body:
Physical joint angles are the sensor/motor angles on the robot. Kinematic angles are the variables used in the idealised model.

They can differ because:

- the zero directions are defined differently;
- the positive rotation direction can be different;
- passive mechanical linkages introduce dependent angles;
- the model can have more kinematic joints than the robot has actuators.

For the Dobot Magician, a parallel linkage keeps the tool axis vertical. Therefore the three physical angles must first be mapped to the model's four kinematic angles before [Forward kinematics](/egb339/temaer/forward-kinematics) is used. — [Assessment 1.3 - Dobot forward kinematics](/egb339/vurderinger/assessment-1-3-dobot-forward-kinematics)

Source: [Assessment 1.3 - Dobot forward kinematics](/egb339/vurderinger/assessment-1-3-dobot-forward-kinematics).
